"Organizes land parcels and their identifiers using the jurisdiction's recorded boundary and tenure information.\n\nUse when: Locate the parcel associated with a land record. Review how adjacent parcels relate in planning or property administration.\n\nAvoid when: A generalized title plan is being used to assert an exact legal boundary without supporting authority.\n\nVisual grammar: Parcel polygons and identifiers connect locations to land records. Boundary marks follow the source jurisdiction's conventions. Scale, survey reference and record status explain the plan's interpretation limits.\n\nBuild: Identify the authoritative parcel source, jurisdiction and record date. Match parcel geometry to stable identifiers and relevant supporting records. Show boundary status and avoid adding unsupported precision or ownership claims.\n\nCheck: Parcel identifiers resolve to the correct source records. Adjacent boundaries preserve recorded topology. General boundaries and legally determined boundaries are distinguished where applicable.\n\nPitfalls: Inferring exact ownership limits from line thickness on a generalized plan. Treating a physical fence as necessarily identical to the recorded boundary.\n\nExample: A planning overview locates three adjoining parcels by their record identifiers and refers precise boundary questions to the authoritative survey materials.\n\nAlternatives: Reference map, Topographic map."
